# An [[appendage]]; something [[attach]]ed to something else in a [[subordinate]] [[capacity]].
#: {{syn|en|addition|supplement|Thesaurus:adjunct}}
#* {{RQ:Marlowe Tamburlaine|part=1|scene=ii|page=22|passage=Lie here ye weedes that I diſdaine to weare,<br>This compleat armor, and this curtle-axe / Are '''adiuncts''' more beſeeming ''Tamburlaine''.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Love's Labour's Lost|act=IV|scene=iii|page=135|passage=Learning is but an '''adiunct''' to our ſelfe,<br>And where we are, our Learning likewiſe is.}}
# A [[person]] [[associate]]d with another, usually in a subordinate [[position]]; a [[colleague]].
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:associate|Thesaurus:attendant}}
#* {{RQ:Wotton Reliquiae|chapter=Essex and Buckingham|page=5|passage=[H]e made him the aſſociate of his Heir apparant, together vvith the nevv Lord ''Cottington'' (as an '''adjunct''' of ſingular experience and truſt) in forraine travailes, and in a buſineſſe of Love, and of no equall hazzard {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|education}} {{ellipsis of|en|adjunct professor}}.
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=March 14 2014|journal=w:The Guardian|title=Life without tenure: how I've found fulfilment in US academia|author=Jacqueline Taylor
|text=I've been given the chance to do this through my own department and through university programmes that don't have tenure-track lines and are therefore more likely to seek assistance from '''adjuncts'''.
|url=https://www.theguardian.com/higher-education-network/blog/2014/mar/14/adjunct-university-united-states-teaching-phd-students-instructor-tenure}}
# {{lb|en|brewing}} An [[unmalted]] [[grain]] or grain product that [[supplement]]s the main [[mash]] ingredient.
# {{lb|en|dated|metaphysics}} A [[quality]] or [[property]] of the body or mind, whether natural or acquired, such as colour in the body or judgement in the mind.
# {{lb|en|music}} A [[key]] or [[scale]] closely related to another as [[principal]]; a relative or attendant key.
# {{senseid|en|grammar}} {{lb|en|grammar}} A [[phrase]] within a [[clause]] or [[sentence]] that is [[grammatical]]ly [[dispensable]] but not [[semantic]]ally so, modifying its meaning.
#: {{hypo|en|modifier<id:grammar>|supplement<id:grammar>}}
#: {{comeronyms|en|head<id:core of phrase, in linguistics>|complement<id:grammar>}}
#: {{coi|en|[[noun adjunct|noun '''adjunct''']]}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=4 April 81|journal=Gay Community News|page=9|author=Signe A. Dayhoff|title=Sexist Language: You Become Your Label|text=When a female enters the profession, she is generally not referred to as ''doctor'' but as a ''lady doctor'' or ''woman doctor''. The use of "feminizing" '''adjuncts''' designates a deviation from the norm, ''doctor'', and does not carry the weight of the term unmodified.}}
# {{lb|en|paleography}} A graphic element that modifies another, such as (in [[Linear B]] script) a small [[syllabogram]] that is attached to a [[logogram]] as an abbreviation of an adjective that modifies that logogram (rather than as a [[w:phonetic complement|phonetic complement]] that disambiguates the logogram).
# {{lb|en|syntax|X-bar theory}} A [[constituent]] which is both the daughter and the sister of an [[X-bar]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1988|author=Andrew Radford| title=Transformational grammar: a first course| location=Cambridge, UK| publisher=Cambridge University Press| page=177
|passage=We can see from (34) that Determiners are sisters of N-bar and daughters of N-double-bar; '''Adjuncts''' are both sisters and daughters of N-bar; and Complements are sisters of N and daughters of N-bar. This means that '''Adjuncts''' resemble Complements in that both are daughters of N-bar; but they differ from Complements in that '''Adjuncts''' are sisters of N-bar, whereas Complements are sisters of N. Likewise, it means that '''Adjuncts''' resemble Determiners in that both are sisters of N-bar, but they differ from Determiners in that '''Adjuncts''' are daughters of N-bar, whereas Determiners are daughters of N-double-bar.}}
# {{lb|en|rhetoric}} [[symploce|Symploce]].
# {{lb|en|category theory}} One of a pair of [[morphism]]s which relate to each other through a pair of [[adjoint functor]]s.

